{
  "header": [
    "ECP-R1-pending-c — over WebSocket, protocol frames are never sent as text-mode messages.",
    "",
    "Batch 2. Second WebSocket obligation under §9.1's \"Wire framing (§1.6)\" row. Routed (F15)."
  ],
  "id_status": "pending-split",
  "under": "ECP-R1",
  "split_index": "c",
  "routed": "docs/status/FINDINGS-FROM-AUTHORING.md F15",
  "title": "Over WebSocket, the peer never sends a protocol frame in a text-mode message",
  "spec": "ENTITY-CORE-PROTOCOL §1.6 (WebSocket transport)",
  "snapshot": "entity-core-protocol/v0.8.2.21",
  "level": "MUST NOT",
  "profile": [
    "core"
  ],
  "surface": "wire",
  "status": "draft",
  "reading": [
    "§1.6: \"Text-mode WebSocket messages MUST NOT be used for protocol frames.\"",
    "",
    "SEPARATE FROM ECP-R1-pending-b BECAUSE IT FAILS INDEPENDENTLY. A peer can send exactly one frame per",
    "message, every time, with the opcode set to text — a JavaScript sender passing a string instead of an",
    "ArrayBuffer does exactly that. It satisfies \"exactly one frame\" and violates this. Conversely a peer",
    "can batch binary messages and never touch text mode. Two failure modes, two files.",
    "",
    "\"USED FOR PROTOCOL FRAMES\" SCOPES THE BAN. It does not forbid text messages on the connection for",
    "other purposes (none are defined), and the suite does not assert that no text message is ever sent —",
    "only that none carries a frame. In practice any text message the peer sends during a protocol",
    "exchange is examined: if its content is a frame (4-byte prefix + CBOR, or any encoding of one), it is",
    "this failure.",
    "",
    "Receiver behaviour on an inbound text frame is not stated and is not asserted.",
    "",
    "VACUOUS without a WebSocket endpoint; SKIP naming it, as in pending-b."
  ],
  "preconditions": {
    "grants": [],
    "identities": [
      "caller"
    ],
    "handlers": [],
    "posture_axes": [
      "transport"
    ],
    "note": "transport = websocket; absent endpoint -> SKIP naming it."
  },
  "arm": [
    {
      "name": "binary-only",
      "kind": "conformant",
      "given": "a WebSocket connection; a handshake and at least one authenticated EXECUTE",
      "why": "§1.6's MUST NOT, over every message the peer sends in a complete exchange.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "ws_open"
        },
        {
          "op": "connect"
        },
        {
          "op": "send_execute",
          "capture": "exchange",
          "uri": "system/tree",
          "operation": "get"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "ws_opcode_of_frame_messages",
          "capture": "exchange",
          "equals": "binary",
          "why": "every message carrying a frame has opcode 0x2."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "control",
      "kind": "negative-control",
      "given": "the suite's recording of a fixture stream carrying one valid frame in a text-mode message",
      "why": "MANDATORY ANTI-VACUITY, suite-side for the same reason as pending-b: a WebSocket client library that normalises opcodes before handing messages up will report 'binary' for everything, and this fixture is what shows the suite reads the opcode off the wire rather than off the library.",
      "step": [
        {
          "op": "replay_fixture",
          "fixture": "one valid frame, opcode 0x1 (text)",
          "capture": "fixture"
        }
      ],
      "assert": [
        {
          "kind": "ws_opcode_of_frame_messages",
          "capture": "fixture",
          "expect": "fails"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "non-conformant-witness",
      "kind": "non-conformant",
      "given": "a browser-hosted peer sending frames via WebSocket.send(string)",
      "expect": "frames arrive as text messages",
      "why": "the most likely real-world violator, named so a suite author knows where to look."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "peer_attributable": true,
    "oracle_check": "",
    "oracle_note": "none in `connectivity`."
  }
}
